The unit solved the real tension (byte-intact AND tagged) with a SIDECAR,
qa/evidence/<slug>/mutations.stale.json, plus a new schema and a doctor-wired check. What makes
it worth checking hard rather than rubber-stamping:
- it does NOT trust a tombstone presence -- it re-resolves moved_to, giving three distinct
  outcomes including "the tag itself lies". That is the AT-218 class answered directly.
- it found and fixed its own false positive first: bare kills names must resolve against the
  spec declared tests scope, not the whole repo. The checker is told to re-derive that against
  scripts/mutation_check.py rather than trust it -- if that semantics is wrong, every bare-name
  resolution in the new check is wrong.
- RETROACTIVE SCOPE IS THE REAL RISK: running the check cold surfaced 27 already-stale kills
  entries across 7 specs, each hand-tombstoned. A wrong moved_to that happens to resolve
  silences a real staleness signal permanently -- strictly worse than the neglect it replaces.
  The checker is asked to spot-check a sample independently.
- it documented a Windows trap I would otherwise have hit: core.autocrlf=true means a naive
  sha256sum vs `git show HEAD:<path>` ALWAYS mismatches even on an untouched file. Byte-intact
  was proved with git hash-object instead.
- one tombstone records moved_by_unit "unknown" with a note rather than a fabricated attribution.

AGAIN DID NOT ACCEPT A done_check AS ACCEPTANCE: T-189 is `pytest -k evidence`, named by
